course: dictionary
lesson: 2810
title: Distillation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Distillation is a way to make a liquid pure. First you heat the liquid until it turns into vapour, which is a gas. Then you cool the vapour so it turns back into a liquid, and you collect that liquid.
explain: Look at this example: «To get pure water from salty water, the vapour must be collected and cooled so it condenses; this is called distillation.» Condenses means the vapour cools and turns back into liquid.
explain: Why does this work with salty water? The water turns into vapour and leaves the salt behind. Only the water comes back as a liquid, so the drops are pure water and the salt stays in the pot.
explain: Distillation uses two steps you already know: evaporation (liquid to vapour) and condensation (vapour to liquid). Check it yourself: the salt did not go up with the water, so it must have stayed behind.
fact: Distillation means heating a liquid, then cooling its vapour back into a liquid and collecting it.
fact: «Ships and some dry countries make drinking water from seawater by distillation and other methods.»
fact: Distilled water is pure water, with the salt and other bits left behind.
fact: Evaporation turns a liquid into vapour; condensation turns vapour back into a liquid.
fact: A distillation can also mean the most important part of something, kept after the rest is taken away.
exercise: To get pure water back from the vapour, what do you do? | Pour it away | *Cool it so it turns back into liquid | Heat it more | Mix it with salt
exercise: «Distillation is boiling a liquid and cooling the steam back to liquid; the drops are pure water, and the salt stays behind.» Where does the salt go? | *It stays behind in the pot | It turns into drops | It sinks into the drops | It goes up with the steam
exercise: «Ships and some dry countries make drinking water from seawater by distillation and other methods.» What is the name of the method that makes seawater safe to drink? | Blowing bubbles into it | Sorting it by colour | *Distillation | Painting it
test: Which two steps happen in distillation, in order? | Condensation, then freezing | Freezing, then melting | Melting, then burning | *Evaporation, then condensation
test: What does «distilled water» mean? | Water from a puddle | *Pure water with the salt and other bits removed | Water with lots of salt in it | Water that is coloured
exam: Why does the salt stay behind during the distillation of salty water? | The salt melts into the cooled drops | *The salt does not turn into vapour, so it is left in the pot | The salt is too heavy to float up | The salt is caught by the lid
